WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.700
Why.

2
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.412
Federal Zero Trust policy
moved the access decision.

3
00:00:04.412 --> 00:00:10.026
OMB memorandum M-22-09 requires agency
staff to use enterprise-managed

4
00:00:10.026 --> 00:00:14.437
identities and phishing-resistant
multifactor authentication.

5
00:00:14.437 --> 00:00:19.841
NIST Special Publication 800-207
removes implicit trust based

6
00:00:19.841 --> 00:00:23.600
on network location: every
request is evaluated on its own.

7
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.050
And CISA's Zero Trust Maturity Model puts
identity first among its five pillars.

8
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.261
What that means in practice is that the

9
00:00:31.261 --> 00:00:34.162
identity provider enforces
whatever it believes.

10
00:00:34.162 --> 00:00:36.838
Who is this person,
and is that still true?

11
00:00:36.838 --> 00:00:39.625
Which groups and roles
do they hold, and why?

12
00:00:39.625 --> 00:00:42.063
Is the device they're
using managed and healthy?

13
00:00:42.063 --> 00:00:46.233
Conditional Access, Privileged
Identity Management and device

14
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:50.250
compliance all read those facts
and act on them, on every sign-in.

15
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:55.375
So if the facts are wrong, every policy
built on them is wrong, at machine speed.

16
00:00:55.375 --> 00:01:00.075
That's why moving from Active Directory
to Entra ID isn't a directory upgrade.

17
00:01:00.075 --> 00:01:04.320
It decides whether every later
control has true facts to act on.
